  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 AM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nA surface ridge of high pressure continues to foster light \nsurface winds as it slides eastward into the western Great Lakes\nregion early this morning. The main concern with this is that \nthe lingering low-level moisture (noted by current dewpoint \ndepressions of 1-3 degrees F) will favor the development of some\nlocally dense fog/and or low clouds prior to daybreak this \nmorning. Currently, it appears the best potential for fog will \nbe primarily in areas along and west of the Fox Valley in \nnorthern IL through about 8 to 9 am. Farther east, slightly \ngreater northeast flow off the surface may favor more of a low \nstratus deck verses true surface fog. While there have been some\nreports of shallow fog in parts of northwestern IL, nothing \nwidespread has yet developed. This may change during the predawn\nhours, so we will continue to monitor observation trends.\n\nAny fog or low clouds that develop early this morning will \nquickly diurnally improve through mid morning. Once it does, we \nwill end up with a rather nice weather day across the region. \nTemperatures are forecast to top out in the low 80s across \ninterior sections of IL under partly cloudy skies. Closer to the\nlake, light northeasterly synoptic flow under the surface high \nwill be reinforced by an afternoon lake breeze, which will hold \nhighs generally in the mid 70s this afternoon. \n\nDry weather continues Tuesday, though temperatures will be a \nbit warmer (low to mid 80s) as the flow turns southwesterly \nalong the western periphery of the eastward exiting surface \nridge, and in advance of a cold front shifting southward across \nthe Upper Midwest. While dry locally Tuesday, forecast attention\nduring the day will be focused on the approaching upstream cold\nfront, and the large upper-level low shifting over southern \nHudson Bay. By midweek, seasonably strong southwesterly mid and \nupper-level flow developing along the southern periphery of the \nHudson Bay low will steer several smaller scale impulses east-\nnortheastward along the surface front as its shifts into our \narea. Accordingly, it appears that we are heading into a period \nof active weather midweek, with good chances (50%+) for showers \nand thunderstorms (some possible strong/severe), particularly \nWednesday into Thursday. Instances of very heavy rainfall and \nrenewed flash flood potential look to be of concern with these \nmidweek storms. This is particularly the case given the presence\nof a very moist prefrontal airmass (precipitable water values \n~2\"), as well as the possibility for repeated rounds of storms \nmoving over the same areas. \n\nA period of quieter weather looks likely late Thursday into \nFriday as the surface cold front gets shunted south. However, \nlow- level WAA and moisture advection will quickly return by \nearly next weekend while a broad low-amplitude ridge drifts \nacross the western CONUS. Expect summerlike warmth and \nincreasing humidity for the weekend. Building instability and \nmodest WNW flow aloft will also support increasing chances for \nconvective episodes into the Great Lakes region by Sunday.\n\nKJB",
